Date: Wed, 29 Dec 1993 20:36:28 -0500 (EST)
From: usr1593a@tso.uc.EDU (Bruce Zimov)
Subject: FOUNDATIONS: Technology/Politics

I definitely got blasted with that one! Let me regroup and fall out of
flame-speak for a second.

Consider this seriously. The goal is to get the technology here as
"efficiently" as possible. I think that any politics, not just a
free market system, could be exploited to minimize the distance between
today and ridiculously long life spans.  Now, even I admit, that there
are limits. We don't want genocide, we don't want totalitarianism, etc.
even if they would shorten the years before we get this technology.
I think don't want these extreme forms, not so much as a general ad hoc
prohibition against evil, immoral politics, rather we don't want these
extreme forms because of the risk that accompanies them.  For example,
in totalitarianism, the whole cause can get railroaded for decades at
the whim or passion of the dictator.  Having said that, I will add that
I endorse the prohibitions against extremely negative, in terms of human
cost, politics NOT just from a risk perspective, but from the moral 
perspective as well. However, as far as any mathematical analysis that
could be done, I think the risk function affects the outcome more than
the moral function.

One clue that this is so is that in all probability we will not have to
deal with extreme political forms at all, INCLUDING totally free market
systems, to actually bring about the technology.  So, I cannot endorse
a monolithic view of how to get from here to there. The freedom of the
market excludes certain rare shortcuts that "the system" as we know it
today can facilitate. Example: Cyberonics is an OTC company marketing
a DIRECT NEURAL/ELECTRONIC LINK using licensed technology that originally
spun off a government NIH grant.  

However, here is the clincher.  Admit that if President Clinton said  
ANYTHING AT ALL publicly either pro or con about cryonics and longevity,
about ALCOR, or what have you, THAT mention alone would bring it to
national attention, and THAT would shorten the day that we have to wait
for incredibly long life spans.  So, it would appear that it is in the
interests of the members of the list to contact CLINTON through Internet
Email, or figure out the most effective means to bring this to the
national spotlight.  One way would be to Internet or contact someone on
the White House Press Corp and solicit their advice on the matter of how
most effectively to bring this to the attention of the nation, instead of
just being a footnote to the California News about weird things at ALCOR.

Now, if that sounds like lobbying, so be it, or you can call it "outreach".
What I am arguing for is the conclusion that it is theoretically effective,
and much more efficient than dreaming about emergence from the ideal free
market system.

