On Tue, 28 Dec 1993, Timothy C. May wrote:

> Phil Fraering writes:
> 
> > I thought I'd forward this to the list, given that others here
> > seem interested in B-5, and it's finally glad to find a science-
> 
> I know about B-1 and B-3, and so forth, but what's the scoop on B-5?
> How much is needed, and how much is too much?

B-5 (pantothenic acid) is as far as I know non-toxic (well as far as that
goes). According to Smart Drugs and Nutrients, in large doses it may
cause diarrhea, but those effects disappear with continued use.

Pantothenic acid has lot's of uses in the body. Powerful antioxidant, good for
stress and plays a vital role in the conversion of choline into
acetylcholine, a neurotransmitter.

The RDA is 10mg. Optimal dosages start around 75-100mg with a normal diet.
If one is taking choline supplements, in a 3-1 ratio by weight (ie. 3g of
choline per day takes 1g of B-5 per day) for conversion into acetylcholine.

There is no natural sign of deficiency for B-5. Foods that you can obtain it
from: Yeast, liver, eggs, kidney, peanut products, rice bran, wheat bran.

This reminds me that I've run out of it (as well as choline)...

