Stuart Card talks about the problems regarding children entering contracts.

I think the problem with young children is the biggest obstacle to my idea
about education and future payback.  I forgot to state in my earlier response
to Tim May's post that I have no problem with his idea about educating ghetto
kids as long as the kids can enter into their own contracts (although this
may have been understood).  Perhaps one way to deal with this problem is to
have to parents commit some of their own future earnings, until the child is
old enough to enter into his/her own contract, at which point the child can
choose to extend the contract and assume the obligations of the parents as
well.  It may be difficult to satisfy the condition that the parents will care
enough to commit the collateral but will not have enough to fund the education
themselves or be intelligent enough to provide some of the education
themselves.  Anyway, I'm not sure how to deal with deciding when children
should be treated as indpendent.  Are there some obvious good solutions that
I am unaware of?
It seems that bright children would be able to make their own decision on the
contract earlier, so that is yet another reason to screen carefully.

